DOCS["krasnow-grating-filter"].update({
    "description": "Create open-path diffraction-grating artwork based on Ben Krasnow's method by using the cut settings stored in the selected material's Holographic entry as a machine-specific 1.00 µm calibration anchor and scaling the output-layer speeds to produce a range of pulse pitches.",
    "intro": "Krasnow Color Grating uses Rasterizer's normal image resizing, palette quantization, and color-region cleanup stages, then rebuilds the non-Black geometry as patches of parallel open lines. Source-image luminance controls each patch's line angle, while source-image hue combined with a generated vertical grayscale correction determines which available non-Black diffraction-grating layer receives that patch.",
    "sections": [
        ("What remains unchanged", ["The source image is resized to the selected processing dimensions, quantized against the available non-Black Rasterizer swatches backed by matching cut settings in the selected material, and cleaned into closed color regions by the normal pipeline. Krasnow Color Grating does not load a separate grating palette, and its specialized handling of source-derived Black geometry does not affect any other filter or image preset."]),
        ("Black geometry", ["Rasterizer separately reduces the resized source image to an adaptive two-color mask, converts the darker portion into merged closed geometry, and places that geometry on the Black layer. This Black geometry remains closed and is not replaced with diffraction-grating lines. The preset works best when Black appears sparingly in the source image, such as outlines or small dark details, rather than large filled regions. The preset does not generate additional Black background geometry or duplicate the non-Black grating paths onto the Black layer; the Black layer contains only the closed geometry produced by the adaptive source-image mask."]),
        ("Open-path geometry", ["Each non-Black color region is divided into local patches, and every patch is rebuilt as parallel open lines clipped to that region's prepared boundary. The defaults match Ben's 0.4 mm patch size and 0.06 mm line spacing. By default, source-image luminance maps from -90 degrees for the darkest values to +90 degrees for the lightest values, matching Ben's grayscale-to-angle convention.", "Every available non-Black grating layer is exported in LightBurn Line mode because the hatch-like pattern has already been constructed as vector paths. The non-Black grating layers therefore contain open paths, while source-derived Black remains closed geometry on the Black layer."]),
        ("Holographic calibration anchor", ["The selected material must contain a Material Library entry whose Description, or whose stored cut setting's internal name, contains \"Holographic.\" Rasterizer treats the speed and frequency stored in the matched Holographic entry as a machine-specific combination that produces a calibrated 1.00 µm pulse pitch (usually an iridescent color setting whose apparent color changes with the lighting or viewing angle, like an oil slick or soap bubble). Rasterizer loads the matched Holographic entry once as a template, then independently copies it onto every available non-Black output layer that remains after normal Rasterizer swatch and Material Library matching.", "Each clone retains its output layer's original LightBurn ID, color, and name. Power, frequency, pulse width, passes, and the other copied laser settings remain equal to the Holographic anchor; only each output layer's speed is adjusted to produce its target pulse pitch."]),
        ("How correction is applied", ["For source colors above the Saturation Cutoff, Rasterizer applies the selected Hue Rotation and converts the rotated hue through Ben Krasnow's inverse 15-to-240 control mapping to produce a grating-layer selection offset. Rasterizer adds the top-to-bottom correction ramp defined by Gradient Top, Gradient Bottom, and Gradient Curve to the hue-derived offset, then limits the combined value to the 0-to-255 range. Rasterizer divides the combined value across however many non-Black grating layers are available and assigns those levels in the layers' native LightBurn order. Source colors at or below the Saturation Cutoff are treated as neutral and receive only the vertical correction ramp, without a hue-derived offset."]),
        ("Pitch extrapolation", ["Pulse pitch is the distance between successive laser pulses along the scan direction and is determined by speed divided by frequency; increasing speed or decreasing frequency increases that distance. With frequency fixed and Speed Spread at its default of 1, each target layer uses: layer speed = Holographic anchor speed × target pitch / 1.00 µm. When multiple non-Black grating layers are available, their target pitches are distributed evenly in LightBurn order from approximately 0.55 to 1.55 µm; if only one is available, it remains at the 1.00 µm anchor.", "Speed Spread controls how far layer speeds diverge from the anchor: speed = anchor speed × [1 + Speed Spread × (target pitch / 1.00 µm − 1)]. A value of 0 gives every layer the anchor speed, 1 preserves the normal calculated range, and 2 doubles each layer's deviation while the 1.00 µm anchor point remains fixed. The control accepts 0 through 2 in 0.001 increments.", "For example, a Holographic anchor of 300 mm/s at 300 kHz produces a 1.00 µm pulse pitch, and a full 21-layer progression at Speed Spread 1 becomes 165, 180, 195, and so on through 465 mm/s.", {"before": "Rasterizer follows the fixed-frequency speed progression demonstrated in Ben Krasnow's ", "link_text": "MOPA Laser Diffraction Gratings reference project", "url": BEN_KRASNOW_DIFFRACTION_REPO, "after": ", then redistributes that progression across however many non-Black grating layers are available."}]),
        ("Calibration", ["Krasnow Color Grating does not require a fixed number of Rasterizer swatches or LightBurn layers. Each available non-Black LightBurn layer carries a separate grating level with its own calculated speed and microscopic pulse pitch. The LightBurn layer colors are organizational identifiers and do not predict the engraved color; the physical diffraction effect is determined by each layer's calculated speed-to-frequency ratio.", "In a Material Library-backed job, source-derived Black geometry remains on the Black LightBurn layer and uses the matched Black cut setting from the selected material. SVG-only output does not apply laser settings or produce a LightBurn project layer. In a Material Library-backed job, the filter leaves the matched Black cut setting's speed, power, frequency, and operation type unchanged."]),
        ("Physical limitations", ["The calculation assumes that the selected Holographic cut setting produces a 1.00 µm pulse pitch with the user's specific laser, lens, material, focus, and pulse behavior. Rasterizer cannot verify that calibration or compensate for nonlinear behavior from the laser, controller, optics, or material. At the default Speed Spread value of 1, calculated layer speeds range from 0.55× to 1.55× the Holographic anchor speed. At the maximum value of 2, they range from 0.10× to 2.10× the anchor speed. Before producing final artwork, verify that every calculated speed is within your machine's operating limits, engrave a small pitch test, and inspect the result using the intended lighting and viewing positions.", "The default Gradient Top and Gradient Bottom values reproduce the approximate 165-to-90 vertical correction used in Ben Krasnow's reference project, but the required correction depends on the artwork's lighting and viewing geometry. Use Gradient Top, Gradient Bottom, and Gradient Curve to tune the correction for the artwork's intended size, orientation, lighting position, and viewing position."]),
    ],
    "related": ["abstract-filters", "color-layers", "diffraction-gratings", "holographic-calibration"],
})

